my very first trip to Africa was in  college I was an undergraduate and I  studied at the University of Nairobi and  had a remarkable experience for me in  many ways life-changing what I saw in  Kenya and also in Uganda and Tanzania  was literally for me breathtaking a  poverty something that shook me to my  core I'll never forget my first time  walking through Mathare Valley in  Nairobi it's seemingly endless slum  fills low rising Hills massive  corrugated metal dirt roads animals and  people crowded in a constantly jumbling  mass it's difficult to imagine unless  you live there or have been there and  while there is poverty in every nation  on earth including our own the depth of  poverty in the slums of the developing  world is really unmatched both re today  is home to more than half a million  kenyans and they live without running  water without sewers without access to  basic health care or education adequate  food or any sort of public safety  services homes are constructed really  from garbage from corrugated tin from  cast-off components of others lives and  the stench of that slum is unbearable  and unforgettable to all but those  forced to live there day in and day out  you cannot in my view really know abject  poverty until you've seen it with your  own eyes and been confronted by its  depth and by its painful persistence  that particular day I saw and in the  months that unfolded after that my own  faith was deeply challenged and shaped  by the experiences that I had in Kenny  had changed the way I looked at my own  country and at the economic disparities  here and between here and the rest of  the world I saw something else though  all across Kenya in places where I  worked urban and rural as a volunteer  both on that trip and when I returned  subsequently which was in striking  contrast to the material poverty of the  folks with whom I visited and that was  just an astonishing openness kindness  gentleness willingness to welcome and  embrace strangers and visitors that was  utterly unexpected and in some ways  humbling I remember my first church  service in the in gong hills at  presbyterian church I grew up in a  wonderfully suburban somewhat frozen  Chosin sort of church in New Castle  County Delaware and the service that I  joined their went on not for an hour not  for two hours not for three hours but  went nearly four hours and was  punctuated repeatedly with spontaneous  demonstrations of joy that weren't just  a few moments long but turned into  entire parades of the congregation  through the whole village dancing and  singing and shouting hallelujah and  praise to God and at one point a woman  sitting next to me turned and said are  you a missionary and I said no but if  you've got any spare moments I'd love it  if you'd come in witness to the church  where i grew up we in our incredible  material wealth desperately needed the  intensity the passion of their  acceptance of and joy for God something  I had not ever seen in that same depth  in my own community where I've been  raised and that challenged my faith that  folks so poor a material wealth were so  in abundant in their love for God and in  their willingness to welcome others it  was heartbreaking to me just how  incredibly gracious and generous these  people could be visiting once a family  in a rural area when I was working with  wanna walk away mondeo they I learned  had slaughtered their very last goat in  order to provide an adequate meal  welcoming strangers from another country  and we were as three strangers arriving  in Abraham's tent something that for me  had resonance with the greatest  traditions of hospitality to strangers  to angels among us from the very most  ancient of times and it was just five  years after these original experiences  in Africa that I spent my time at the  Yale Divinity School and it helped me  explore the questions that had been  raised for me in the context of my faith  and I am thrilled tonight to be able to  join with scholars and friends with  fellow alumni for this important  dialogue concerning many of these same  issues at the intersection of faith and  poverty I want to thank Dean Attridge  and the other organizers for inviting me  to participate and the press club for  hosting us this evening today is of  course Ash Wednesday and we stand  tonight on the threshold of Lent a  season hopefully for all of us in  different ways of reflection of  repentance end of sharper focus  lent should be a time when we consider  the number and significance of our good  works of our open-hearted responses to  God's calling in God's grace and when we  measure the seriousness of our sacrifice  which in many ways are the truest  measures of ourselves as I prepared for  this visit with you tonight I thought a  great deal about that time now more than  25 years ago when I first spent months  in Africa but as much about what  happened to me after I returned to the  US as when I was actually there in the  two or three years following my initial  return from Kenya I was shall we say a  constant reminder to everyone around me  of the troubles facing that continent  whether they really wanted to hear it or  not seeing the poverty of Mathare II and  other places in East Africa lit the fire  of activism within me and drove me to  become ceaselessly involved as only the  young really can as an activist for  human rights and for poverty relief and  I spent the last year of college and  then two years afterwards dedicated to  the fight against apartheid first in  Washington and then with the South  African council of churches and I then  returned after that to Kenyan to Uganda  to do some relief work with the  presbyterian church before ultimately  deciding after a lengthy late night  conversation with a priest friend who is  today the Bishop of Johannesburg to  return to the country from which I came  and try and work individually to  confront poverty at its most abject here  I went to working with the homeless in  New York City and found in the homeless  shelters of Fort Washington and over the  five states where I went in the year  that followed with the National  Coalition for the homeless a similarly  deeply challenging exposure to what  abject poverty means in this richest of  countries but also incredibly  challenging moments of grace of profound  witness of closeness to what I believe  to be the Living God the neighbor that  we are called to but so often failed to  really see in those who are all around  us it was my time at Yale but help me  understand the connection between my  faith and God and the effect my  experiences in Africa and with the  homeless had in motivating me to work  not just for charity or for relief but  for justice and reconciliation  while at Yale I was a student intern a  pastor with the downtown cooperative  ministry in New Haven one of America's  poorest and most challenged cities and  it was an opportunity there for me to  see a multi-faith congregation coalition  working to try and address issues of  housing of homelessness in the context  of a city that is also home to one of  the nation's best endowed universities  it was after that time that I returned  home to Wilmington Delaware into the  state where I grew up and I chose as the  place to worship an inner-city church  that was not just actively welcoming to  the homeless but actually is today a  full-time homeless shelter a church West  Presbyterian so dedicated to mission  that it literally succeeded in spending  itself out of existence I served on the  session and will not soon forget the day  when our accountant and chief finance  officer said to us if we continue on the  path we're on of relentless dedication  to mission we will close our doors in  less than five years and the session  voted unanimously to continue and today  the church is closed and it's been  repurposed as a homeless shelter which  was in many ways what is initially  intended to be a place of grace and  respite for those most trouble among us  we really can't predict the journey of  our lives we really can't imagine how  things shown to us early on might  someday come back to us as a challenge  or an opportunity the years I spent in  Africa and working with the homeless in  New York and elsewhere were things that  I largely put aside for the decade that  I was raising three young children  working as in-house counsel the gore-tex  company and then beginning to get  involved in improving my local community  but ultimately in my first elected role  as County Executive responsible for  housing programs for half a million  people I returned to active and engaged  work with Habitat for Humanity with the  faith community in Delaware with the  local government that had turned away  from being engaged in providing safe  sanitary and decent housing and I found  those early formative experiences came  back powerfully and was a tremendous  opportunity for me to work with the  faith community in Delaware and with  government and to find effective relief  for the poor through housing I believe  life for us isn't  acta process of hearing and at times  ignoring what God is calling us to do  and of discerning how best to act upon  that call whether as an individual or as  a nation and with the help of the  guidance I received at the Divinity  School I think I've learned to recognize  that that strong and unique sense of  fulfillment that comes by witnessing  with and working with in partnership not  in charity those who suffer whether  individually the homeless in this  country or an entire continent and  finding ways to forge a closeness with  God through that I hope many of you here  know that same sense that same unusual  and profound feeling of getting out of  the boundaries of our professional lives  of our roles and instead seeing others  as they truly are as equal in creation  so in thinking about tonight and in  thinking about the onset of Lent I  realized that more than 25 years had  passed since my first visits to Africa  and frankly decades have passed since  active personal engagement with the  struggles with the poverty of many of  the developing nations of Africa had  been an important part of my life I  spent as I've mentioned the last 10  years of my life raising three young  children and helping build a business  and helping grow a career so it is  wholly appropriate that's a night's  event like the title of the journal  connected to it is reflections and if  you'll forgive me I've been reflecting  on the very circumstances that in a  roundabout way seem system at least  unexpectedly to have led me from being  the county executive of a small County  in Delaware to being not just a freshman  senator but the newly selected chair of  the Senate Subcommittee on African  affairs after 25 years of focusing on so  many other avenues for service I find  myself unexpectedly drawn back to a  place where work in partnership of the  faith community on Africa not in charity  but in partnership is going to be in  many ways the central call of my work in  the next few years as a person of faith  I find this striking perhaps even  providential interestingly i began my  day this morning at the Senate's weekly  prayer breakfast where joe lieberman  gave moving reflection about the nature  of sabbath he had an interesting comment  he quoted the hebrew  HUD ha Mon excuse me common who said  more than the Jewish people has kept the  Sabbath the Sabbath has kept the Jewish  people just as the Sabbath provides  observant Jews with a time for  reflection and much as Ramadan calls on  Muslims to focus on fasting prayer and  the pursuit of good works Lent provides  those of us in the Christian community  with a chance to repent and reflect on  the direction of our lives on my way  into the Senate buildings this morning  from Union Station I walked past a woman  it's just over 20 or so years ago I  worked with Mitch Schneider on the issue  of homelessness here in Washington some  of you may remember Mitch now long gone  there is still a shelter here which  bears Mitch's name I last saw him  literally the day before he became a  victim of suicide when he was testifying  in front of the DC City Council in his  usual passionate and endless way about  the importance of dealing with the  homeless there is a woman who sits at  the foot of the fountain in front of  union station who has been there every  day that I've come to Washington for 20  years and I realized as a senator I have  walked briskly past her virtually every  day of my last three months some of you  know her she has a face painted wildly  and tiger stripes she sits in rags and  often actually smiles and greets folks  who walked by her today I stopped and  said hello and simply tried to give her  that moment of equality of recognition  of someone who deserves my attention as  I've taken from hers it was a simple and  small act of kindness but lent I think  begins with such steps as these a  challenge towards basic human decency to  stop on the pathway that seems so set  for us so paved so predictable and to  reflect on where we've gone perhaps a  little astray I've always been awed by  Americans by our stunning generosity by  our capacity to be lifted up out of our  chairs by what we see on our televisions  of the suffering of the farthest corners  of the world whether it's the disasters  of the tsunami or the challenges of  those in Sudan Americans in response to  great natural disasters are  astonishingly generous hundreds of  millions of dollars poured to Haiti and  really  within hours of the worst earthquake in  modern times yet i'm also overcome by  our capacity for casual almost brutal  indifference to the suffering of those  in the grips of abject poverty that we  know exists in our world each and every  day most Americans know that roughly  20,000 children die every single day  worldwide from completely preventable  diseases that just literally for pennies  each could be cured and we spend more as  a people every day on soft drinks and  video games than any of us individually  do or than we collectively as a nation  do on tending to the poor and on feeding  the hungry and the homeless around the  world so I think my challenge as a  freshman senator is to not ever forget  as I have at times forgotten over the  last 25 years the challenge of the  poverty I saw before me in matheran the  pathway that I saw through it and the  challenges that I see for us today in  the United States the power of Lent I  think is that it is a moment for us to  change direction we as a country we as a  global community of those dedicated to  seeing others as neighbor and caring for  them have made real progress the  Millennium Development Goals give us a  measurable way to say that we have made  progress but we also at this moment have  to stop and reflect and say it is not  yet enough we are called we are  challenged as an exercise of our faith  to do more to be more engaged to be more  persistent to not forget and to not turn  back every major faith tradition teaches  that it is our calling our challenge not  just to see those who are poor among us  but to prefer them to act for them from  Isaiah to Luke from the Quran to the  Torah there are passages that challenge  each and every one of us regardless of  your tradition or background to put not  just some time aside for the poor to act  not just in charity but in a way that  focuses on justice on reconciliation and  on development I'm conscious of the  passage of time and want to get to the  panel I have many more reflection  in front of me but I think it might be  best for me to move for the reflections  of the panel I'm conscious that in North  Africa today and in places around the  world there's a dramatic movement  towards liberty towards democracy  towards change in countries that have  long sweltered under dictatorship and  I'm conscious that we can't really make  sustainable progress in tackling poverty  until we tackle development and  development is something that can only  be tackled in partnership with people  whose capacity to be free is supported  by those of us who believe that as a  free nation we have a remarkable model  to provide them I do think that in the  Senate at this moment we have an  enormous challenge just today on the  floor we voted down HR 1 which was the  House Republicans proposed budget it  makes dramatic and broad cuts in  spending of all types spending that  would improve the security and the  quality of life for our communities in  the United States and it would  significantly cut our investment in aid  and relief overseas at a time when  frankly far less than 1% of our total  federal budget less than thirty nine  billion dollars is dedicated to foreign  assistance development and relief I  think it's incumbent on all of us in the  faith community and around the country  to take a step back and to focus on the  long-term on the consequences for our  relationships with countries around the  world and our relationship with  ourselves and our faith if we were to  turn aside from the path that most of  the industrialized world has long been  on with us of seeking collaboratively to  lift up those who we are called to  consider as neighbor not just here but  around the world as i said we as a  nation have a amazing capacity to be  selfless and to be engaged in meeting  the real needs of others and we  similarly have a capacity to turn aside  and to focus narrowly on ourselves as we  come out of the worst recession in  modern memory it is completely  understandable that people all across  this country have pressed back hard  against commitments to foreign  assistance have questioned and  challenged whether the United States  government should be engaged in this at  all and some can say as some will that  perhaps giving by the faith community is  inevitably more effective more sustained  and more positive than the sometimes  of motivated investments and  contributions of the American government  the American people in my view that's a  dialogue in which we must together be  vigorously engaged it is only by hearing  broadly from the people of faith all  across this country that the people of  Congress your representatives and the  folks in our administration can know the  value that we place as a country in  investing in hearing the call that comes  from all of our religious traditions to  view others as neighbors and to care for  those neighbors as we would ourselves  and to lift them bless you out of the  abject poverty that so dominates the  lives of literally billions of our  fellow people as this day for me draws  to a close I can't help but conclude by  thinking about what the spirit of Lent  has been teaching to me today my day  began with Senator Lieberman's comments  about the Sabbath and the need for a  moment of reflection and the real  physical challenge of nearly walking  past a homeless woman who's been in my  path in different ways for much of the  last 20 years I move tomorrow to my  service as a senator and the challenge  of representing you in the fight to  sustained engagement with the whole  continent of Africa I ask for your help  as you go home tonight after this great  panel reflects digests discusses what  small concrete step will you take on  what path will you head what very real  investment or sacrifice are you willing  to make to see those who suffer and  struggle around the world not just as  objects of charity but as are very real  partners in transformation in  development in growth in faith and in  peace I hope we can begin that journey  together god bless

