ly make  sustainable progress in tackling poverty  until we tackle development and  development is something that can only  be tackled in partnership with people  whose capacity to be free is supported  by those of us who believe that as a  free nation we have a remarkable model  to provide them I do think that in the  Senate at this moment we have an  enormous challenge just today on the  floor we voted down HR 1 which was the  House Republicans proposed budget it  makes dramatic and broad cuts in  spending of all types spending that  would improve the security and the  quality of life for our communities in  the United States and it would  significantly cut our investment in aid  and relief overseas at a time when  frankly far less than 1% of our total  federal budget less than thirty nine  billion dollars is dedicated to foreign  assistance development and relief I  think it's incumbent on all of us in the  faith community and around the country  to take a step back and to focus on the  long-term on the consequences for our  relationships with countries around the  world and our relationship with  ourselves and our faith if we were to  turn aside from the path that most of  the industrialized world has long been  on with us of seeking collaboratively to  lift up those who we are called to  consider as neighbor not just here but  around the world as i said we as a  nation have a amazing capacity to be  selfless and to be engaged in meeting  the real needs of others and we  similarly have a capacity to turn aside  and to focus narrowly on ourselves as we  come out of the worst recession in  modern memory it is completely  understandable that people all across  this country have pressed back hard  against commitments to foreign  assistance have questioned and  challenged whether the United States  government should be engaged in this at  all and some can say as some will that  perhaps giving by the faith community is  inevitably more effective more sustained  and more positive than the sometimes  of motivated investments and  contributions of the American government  the American people in my view that's a  dialogue in which we must together be  vigorously engaged it is only by hearing  broadly from the people of faith all  across this country that the people of  Congress your representatives and the  folks in our administration can know the  value that we place as a country in  investing in hearing the call that comes  from all of our religious traditions to  view others as neighbors and to care for  those neighbors as we would ourselves  and to lift them bless you out of the  abject poverty that so dominates the  lives of literally billions of our  fellow people as this day for me draws  to a close I can't help but conclude by  thinking about what the spirit of Lent  has been teaching to me today my day  began with Senator Lieberman's comments  about the Sabbath and the need for a  moment of reflection and the real  physical challenge of nearly walking  past a homeless woman who's been in my  path in different ways for much of the  last 20 years I move tomorrow to my  service as a senator and the challenge  of representing you in the fight to  sustained engagement with the whole  continent of Africa I ask for your help  as you go home tonight after this great  panel reflects digests discusses what  small concrete step will you take on  what path will you head what very real  investment or sacrifice are you willing  to make to see those who suffer and  struggle around the world not just as  objects of charity but as are very real  partners in transformation in  development in growth in faith and in  peace I hope we can begin that journey  together god bless
 

